   "date": "2017-07-25T13:55:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: pcountOpen : problem when adding covariates for abundance",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Clemence,<div><br></div><div>For dynamic models like the Dail-Madsen (pcountopen) or dynamic occupancy models (colext), the site-specific state variable (N, in this case) can initially be modeled (primary occasion 1) as a function of covariates, but is subsequently a function of the parameters describing the dynamics...in your case, with the &#39;trend&#39;  formulation:</div><div><br></div><div>N[i,1]~Poisson(lambda[i])</div><div>N[i, 2]~Poisson(N[i, 1] * gamma[i, 1]). [Or maybe some alternative distribution].</div><div><br></div><div>Because of the way the model likelihood is set up, you can&#39;t directly model variability in N as related to covariates each year; instead, you can model variability in gamma for each primary period*site after the first period. One workaround is to &quot;stack the data&quot; and fit a standard Nmix model (pcount) to each primary period *site combination. I think the first example of doing so is here (using BUGS):</div><div> </div><div>Kery, M., Dorazio, R., Soldaat, L., Van Strien, A., Zuiderwijk, A., and Royle, J. (2009), “Trend estimation in populations\r\nwith imperfect detection,” Journal of Applied Ecology, 46, 1163–1172.</div><div><br></div><div>If you search for &quot;stack or stacking data&quot; on the list, you should find plenty of further descriptions applicable to analysis in unmarked.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div><br></div><div>John</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
